  "body": "Part of #4023. Step K2 of the record (K0, #4024; in `RECOMMENDED.md` under the heading `#4024`, migration order). It is delivered on `side/path-kernel` (AGENTS.md **Side branch**). It builds on K1 (#4025, the region model).\n\n## What to do\n\nAdd the guide stage to `camcraft-pathkernel`: the planners that turn a region into guide curves, behind the trait `GuidePlanner`.\n\n- The guide families of the record: rings (offsets of the region, Held 1991), rows, the medial line, the trace of a chain, and the helix.\n- A guide planner reads only the region and its guide choice. It does not read the start side (law 2 and law 3).\n- The same region and the same choice give the same curves (law 1). The set order is the canonical order of the crate.\n- A planner that cannot make its family says so with a named fallback or a refusal (law 4). It never returns a different family without a name.\n- The ring planner covers the reports of #3750 (one shared ring set) and #3788 (full-width cuts where the rings of two lobes merge): a ring that would be cut at full width is named, so that the load stage (K3) and the link stage (K4) can act on it.\n- Write each planner from published literature. Copy no code path of the old planners that has a known defect; where the old ring code is correct, a port with its provenance is permitted.\n- No old planner changes. With the default switch the output is identical.\n\n## Pins\n\n- Each guide planner is registered in the law suite (#4027) and passes L1, L2, L3 and L4.\n- The ring planner on the disc, the dog bone and the `fig8.step` outline: the rings agree with the recorded old baseline (#4153) within the tolerance, where the old rings are correct. Each difference is named with its reason.\n- Negative tests through the public API for each refusal.\n\n## Done means\n\nThe guide stage is on the side branch with its pins green. K4 (order and link) and the first recipe can then start.\n",
